Source: boswas-bootsplash
Section: admin
Priority: optional
Maintainer: Boswas OS Platform Team <os-platform@boswas.invalid>
Build-Depends: debhelper-compat (= 13), cargo, fonts-inter, rustc (>= 1.85)
Standards-Version: 4.7.2
Rules-Requires-Root: no

Package: boswas-bootsplash
Architecture: amd64
Depends: initramfs-tools,
         ${misc:Depends},
         ${shlibs:Depends}
Conflicts: plymouth
Description: Boswas OS boot, disk-unlock and shutdown screen
 The whole Boswas OS boot screen as one small program written in Rust: the
 Boswas OS mark, which assembles itself as the screen opens, on a dark
 background lit softly in the brand's blues, with a progress ring under it
 while the system starts; the passphrase prompt for an encrypted disk, which
 slides in as the mark glides up; and the shutdown and restart screens, all
 in the Fluent design language of the Boswas desktop. It takes the place of
 Plymouth.
 .
  * boswas-bootsplashd draws directly on the display (kernel mode setting
    with dumb buffers, or a legacy frame buffer), at the display's refresh
    rate, and redraws only what changed; a frame of the ring touches a few
    thousand pixels
  * the mark and the glyphs of the typeface (Inter) are built into the
    program as vector outlines, with the colours, and drawn at the exact
    size of the display: nothing is parsed or loaded at boot
  * the passphrase is read from the console keyboard with the console's
    keymap, kept in one buffer that is wiped after use, and never logged
  * once running, the daemon gives up its privileges: no_new_privs, a
    Landlock domain limited to the graphics devices, the console and its
    runtime directory, no TCP, three capabilities
  * it is started in the initramfs, so the disk passphrase prompt uses it,
    and it answers later password requests through a systemd password agent
 .
 If the boot screen is disabled ("nosplash", or no "splash" on the kernel
 command line) or cannot start, the passphrase is asked for on the text
 console as usual; if it ends abnormally, a supervising process restores the
 text console.
 .
 Inside the initramfs the client is also available as /bin/plymouth, the name
 Debian's disk-unlock helper and the initramfs scripts call. No file named
 plymouth is installed on the root file system, and this package conflicts
 with plymouth so that only one boot screen is in the initramfs.
